To make the stack visualization update automatically when elements are added or removed, you need to implement an **observable stack** that notifies the UI of changes. Here are several approaches:

## Approach 1: Custom Observable Stack (Recommended)

Create a wrapper class that extends `Stack` and adds listener support:

```java
import javafx.application.Platform;
import javafx.scene.control.TextArea;
import java.util.Stack;

public class ObservableStack<T> extends Stack<T> {
    private final TextArea textArea;
    private Runnable onChangeCallback;

    public ObservableStack(TextArea textArea) {
        this.textArea = textArea;
    }

    public void setOnChangeCallback(Runnable callback) {
        this.onChangeCallback = callback;
    }

    @Override
    public T push(T item) {
        T result = super.push(item);
        notifyChange();
        return result;
    }

    @Override
    public synchronized T pop() {
        T result = super.pop();
        notifyChange();
        return result;
    }

    @Override
    public synchronized void clear() {
        super.clear();
        notifyChange();
    }

    private void notifyChange() {
        if (onChangeCallback != null) {
            Platform.runLater(() -> {
                onChangeCallback.run();
                updateVisualization();
            });
        } else {
            Platform.runLater(this::updateVisualization);
        }
    }

    private void updateVisualization() {
        textArea.clear();
        if (isEmpty()) return;

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            sb.append(get(i).toString()).append("\n");
        }
        textArea.setText(sb.toString());
    }
}
```

